            <description><![CDATA[<h2>Jarvis 0.13.0</h2>
<p><b>Join the call from your day.</b> Every meeting on your Focus calendar is
now a button. Click one and Jarvis shows you who is in it, which account it
came from, and a Join button that opens the call — no hunting through the
invite. Where two meetings overlap and share a band, each gets its own Join.</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Only a real link.</b> Jarvis opens the conferencing link the organiser
      set, or a room the invite points at directly. It will not guess from an
      agenda document buried in the description, and a meeting with no link
      simply has no button rather than a dead one.</li>
  <li><b>When your calendar cannot be read, it says so.</b> A calendar that is
      disconnected, or an account whose access has lapsed, now shows on the day
      itself with a way to fix it — and on the Gmail account it belongs to.
      Mail and calendar can fail independently, and one silently empty calendar
      used to look exactly like a day with no meetings.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Catch up on everything, now.</b> Jarvis looks for new work when you open
it and when you come back to it — not on a clock ticking in the background. So
there is a key for asking it to look right now, and it is the shortest one
Jarvis has.</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>⌘J catches up</b>, from any screen — your day, your sources, a task
      you have open. Mail, Slack, new tasks and finished ones, all in one
      press. ⌘⇧S still does the same thing.</li>
  <li><b>⌥⌘J does it from anywhere else.</b> Press it while you are in Slack,
      your browser or your editor and Jarvis catches up in the background
      without taking your screen.</li>
  <li><b>Tell Jarvis has moved to ⌘K</b> — the key command bars use everywhere
      else. The ＋ on Focus still opens it, and the box itself now shows the
      new key.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
